**No. Android doesn't keep a list of failed unlock attempts, and it never shows who made them.** It counts wrong PIN, pattern and password guesses only to make the person wait longer before trying again. Once the phone is unlocked, there's nothing to look back at: no list, no time, no photo. To see failed attempts, you need an app that's notified each time an unlock fails, like [CrookCatcher](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.harteg.crookcatcher&referrer=utm_source%3Dwebsite%26utm_medium%3Dguide%26utm_campaign%3Dfailed-unlock-attempts-android), which takes a photo of the person and emails it to you with the location.

## What Android does with a wrong PIN

Every wrong guess is counted, and after a few of them the lock screen makes the person wait. On recent Android versions (Android 16 QPR2 and later), Google's published schedule is:

| Wrong guesses | Wait before the next try |
| --- | --- |
| 1 to 4 | None |
| 5 | 1 minute |
| 6 | 5 minutes |
| 7 | 15 minutes |
| 8 | 30 minutes |
| 10 | 4 hours |
| 12 | 36 hours |
| 15 | 41 days |
| 20 | No more guesses allowed |

Google says Android 17 uses even stronger limits by default, and phone makers can set their own. Samsung's One UI 9 permanently locks a phone on the 13th wrong guess; after that, only a factory reset makes it usable again.

Two more things changed recently:

- **Repeating the same wrong PIN doesn't count again** on many newer phones. Android and Samsung both skip identical wrong entries, so a child pressing 1111 over and over won't lock you out.
- **Failed Authentication Lock** (Android 15 and later, on by default) locks the screen when authentication fails repeatedly.

None of this is shown to you afterwards. The count only exists to time the lockouts.

## The only clues Android gives you

If someone tried while you were away, you might notice:

- **A "Try again in…" message** on the lock screen when you pick up your phone.
- **A warning about remaining attempts.** Samsung phones with One UI 9 show this once someone gets close to the limit.

These tell you *something* happened. They don't tell you when, how many times, or who.

## What Android can't tell you

- **Who it was.** No photo, no name.
- **When it happened**, or how many attempts were made.
- **Where the phone was.**
- **An alert while it happens.** You find out only if you notice the clues above.

## How to see who tried to unlock your phone

Android does let apps know about failed attempts. An app you give the **Device admin** permission is notified by the system each time someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password. CrookCatcher uses exactly that.

When an unlock fails, CrookCatcher:

- takes a photo with the front camera,
- records the GPS location and an approximate address,
- saves it in the app with the time, and
- emails it all to you, so you have it even if the phone is gone.

