You choose how many wrong attempts it takes before CrookCatcher acts. It has been downloaded more than 12 million times since 2014, and it takes around 1.2 million intruder photos a day. Setup takes a few minutes and there's no account to create: see [how to see who tried to unlock your phone](/blog/see-who-tried-to-unlock-your-phone) for the step-by-step.

## Turn on Android's own protection too

CrookCatcher tells you who tried. Android's theft protection makes it harder for them to succeed. Turn both on.

**Go to:**
`Settings > Google > All services > Theft protection`

Check that **Failed Authentication Lock**, **Theft Detection Lock** and **Offline Device Lock** are on. If a setting is grayed out, your phone doesn't support it. For the full list, see the [top 5 Android settings to secure your phone against theft](/blog/top_five_settings).

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Q: Is there a log of failed unlock attempts on Android?
A: No. Android only keeps a counter for the lockout timer, and it isn't shown anywhere. To get a record, you need an app like CrookCatcher that saves each attempt with a photo, time and location.

### Q: How many times can you enter a wrong PIN on Android?
A: On recent Android versions, the first 4 wrong guesses have no wait. After that the waits grow from 1 minute to days, and after 20 wrong guesses no more are allowed. Samsung phones with One UI 9 lock permanently on the 13th wrong guess.

### Q: Will my phone erase itself after too many wrong attempts?
A: Not by default on most phones. Some Samsung phones have an optional **Auto factory reset** setting that erases the phone after a number of wrong attempts. If a phone runs out of guesses, Google's help pages say the only way back in is to erase it.

### Q: Does entering the same wrong PIN twice count?
A: On many newer phones, no. Android 16 QPR2 and later, and Samsung's One UI 9, ignore repeated identical wrong entries so you don't get locked out by accident.

### Q: Can an app see failed unlock attempts?
A: Yes, if you give it permission. Android notifies apps that have the Device admin permission whenever an unlock attempt fails. That's how CrookCatcher knows when to take a photo. It only uses the permission for that.

### Q: Do failed fingerprint or face unlock attempts count?
A: CrookCatcher reacts to a wrong PIN, pattern or password, not to failed fingerprint or face unlock attempts.

